    "title": "West Michigan native is first woman on moon mission",
    "text": "GRAND RAPIDS, Mich. (WOOD) — As the clock ticked toward liftoff of a historic space mission, one West Michigan aunt still could not believe what her niece was about to embark upon. Christina Koch is among four astronauts that are a part of NASA’s Artemis II mission that took off Wednesday. She is a West Michigan native, born in Sparta. Koch’s aunt, Loretta Homrich, spoke to News 8 over the phone the morning of the scheduled launch, filled with emotions. “When she takes off, it’s going to be heart-stopping,” she said. “This one feels very different because they’re going on the other side of the moon where nobody’s been, so it’s a little scary.” Video Player is loading.Christina Koch's family excited for Artemis II launch Christina Koch's family excited for Artemis II launch NOW PLAYINGChristina Koch's family excited for Artemis II launch Not only is the mission making history, but Koch is as well — she is the first woman to travel to the moon. Hearing that statement is still something Homrich cannot wrap her head around. “She’s always wanted to go out to space and she’s the (woman who has spent the longest time in space) and now she’s going to be the first woman around the moon,” she said. “I’m, like, trembling inside, because just to think about it, it just makes you in awe of everything.” Homrich has been in Florida since the first scheduled launch was delayed, so the anticipation has been building for months. “We were very proud of her,” she said. “She’s just always loved being an astronaut. She always wanted to be an astronaut since she was little. She went to space camp and she just had a wonderful journey.” Hoping for a smooth mission and a safe return home, Homrich cannot wait to talk to and see her niece when she returns. “I’m hoping everything goes well. There’s no hiccups and it’s just a smooth ride and they make it around the moon how they’re supposed to and just come back safely.” *Correction: A previous version of this article misspelled Homrich’s name. We regret the error, which has been fixed.",
